    templates/fonts/OFL-<family>.txt.

selftest/proef.opus — the sentence the self-test speaks
    Not third-party and nobody's voice: made with espeak-ng, whose output is not a recording
    of a person. See selftest/README.md for the command that produced it.

frontend/public/icon.svg, the Preekstof name and mark
    Not third-party. Copyright Merijn Kersten, covered by LICENSE, and not granted for use
    apart from this software.


IN THE DOWNLOADS FOR WINDOWS AND THE MAC
----------------------------------------

Preekstof-Windows.zip, Preekstof-Mac.zip and Preekstof-Mac-Intel.zip carry a Python and the
packages below, already installed, in the folder python/. The plain zip carries neither; its
first start installs the packages with pip, onto the church's own computer.

Python 3.12
    PSF License. Windows: the embeddable build from python.org. Mac: python-build-standalone
    (github.com/astral-sh/python-build-standalone), which bundles a few libraries of its own
    under their own licences (OpenSSL, SQLite, zlib and others). The licence text travels
    with it, in python/LICENSE.txt on Windows and python/lib/python3.12/LICENSE.txt on a Mac.

  fastapi                 MIT
  uvicorn                 BSD 3-Clause
  starlette               BSD 3-Clause
  pydantic                MIT
  python-multipart        Apache 2.0
  faster-whisper          MIT
  ctranslate2             MIT
  onnxruntime             MIT
  numpy                   BSD 3-Clause
  tokenizers              Apache 2.0
  huggingface-hub         Apache 2.0
  anthropic               MIT
  yt-dlp                  Unlicense (public domain)
  pip                     MIT
  mlx-whisper             MIT, Apple Silicon only, optional, requirements-mac.txt; not in the download

These are the ones the app asks for; what they need in turn (httpx, anyio, certifi, tqdm and
the like) comes along. Each package carries its own licence text, and those of the libraries
it bundles, in its *.dist-info folder under python/. The exact versions are in that folder
too; this list names the projects, not a lockfile.

Left out on purpose, and fetched on the church's own computer at the first start:

PyAV (av)
    BSD 3-Clause for PyAV itself, but its wheels carry an FFmpeg built with x264 and x265,
    which are GPL. That is the same reason FFmpeg itself is never in a download (below). The
    download names the version it was built against in bundle.json and the launcher installs
    exactly that one with pip.


FETCHED ON FIRST USE, ONTO THE CHURCH'S OWN COMPUTER
----------------------------------------------------

vision/person.onnx — YOLOv10n person detection
    AGPL-3.0. From huggingface.co/onnx-community/yolov10n.

    Kept out of this repository on purpose: the AGPL reaches into what it is distributed
    with, and shipping it would pull this whole app under that licence. The app downloads it
    on the machine that uses it, the way it already downloads FFmpeg, and passes it on to
    nobody. Following the speaker also works with face.onnx alone, at lower coverage, for
    anyone who would rather not have that licence anywhere near them. See vision/README.md.

Whisper speech models (openai/whisper-* via Systran/faster-whisper-*, or
mlx-community/whisper-*-mlx on an Apple Silicon Mac)
    MIT, from Hugging Face. Downloaded once and cached under the user's Hugging Face folder.

FFmpeg and ffprobe
    Downloaded by the launcher when the machine has none. The Windows build comes from
    gyan.dev and the macOS builds, one for each kind of chip, from ffmpeg.martin-riedl.de.
    FFmpeg is LGPL-2.1-or-later, and those particular builds include GPL components; they are
    executed as separate programs and never linked into this software, and none of the
    downloads above carries them. Source: ffmpeg.org.


SERVICES THIS APP TALKS TO
--------------------------

Anthropic (api.anthropic.com)
    The written-out text of the preaching, to find the moments. Under the church's own API
    key and Anthropic's commercial terms. No audio or video is ever sent. See PRIVACY.md.

Kerkdienstgemist (kerkdienstgemist.nl, and its media on amazonaws.com)
    Only when a church fills in its station number: the list of its own public services and
    the recording behind the site's own download button, requested the same way a visitor's
    browser requests it. The list carries a still of each service, which the browser loads
    from the platform's storage on S3 exactly as that church's own page does. Once a service
    is taken in, the app saves its own copy of that one still and never asks again.

Hugging Face (huggingface.co)
    Model downloads only, listed above.

Nothing is sent to the author of this software. There is no telemetry.
